welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Mark Turan an associate research  scientist at Yale University and  director of the digital Himalaya project  and world or a literature project  professor Turin is an anthropologist and  a linguist his scholarly focus is on the  Himalayan region in particular Nepal  northern India Bhutan and cultural tibet  professor tyrrans research interests  include the documentation of endangered  languages and mapping global cultural  diversity language policy and the role  of native tongue instruction in  education and issues relating to the  electronic access and ownership of  anthropological materials from  ethnographic museums today we talk with  him about his involvement in the world  or a literature project welcome  professor turn I'm delighted to be here  let's begin with an overview of your  project tell us about it the project was  established in January 2009 based at the  University of Cambridge where I work as  a partnership between anthropologists  linguists community members and people  involved in collections archivists and  librarians and people in museums as an  urgent global initiative to come  together and work to preserve protect  document and then disseminate these  incredibly diverse forms of oral  tradition oral literature that exist  around the world how did you become  involved well I've been working in the  Himalayas for almost 20 years now from  1991 onwards and as an anthropologist  and a linguist I've been working  particularly with two ethnic groups and  their languages Nepal is a country of 27  million people but home to about 106  languages incredible linguistic  diversity from four different language  families in fact the Himalayan belt is  really a kind of mega center of  diversity in all senses ecological  environmental culture  linguistic so my own work has been  predicated really on a deep  understanding and appreciation of the  linguistic forms that that make people  who they are and give them a kind of  cultural attachment their own identity I  should add that of the world 6500  languages probably up to half are  endangered Wow and most of those  languages themselves are also oral  primarily transmitted orally so we have  to think about most of the world's  linguistic and cultural diversity being  transmitted by word of mouth and what  are the goals of the project the project  is doing a number of things to support  both communities and also careful  documentation projects I like to use the  words collect protect and connect to  describe a little bit what we do  collection is not the acquisition in a  kind of colonial or acquisitive manner  of materials but rather the careful  partnering with people who have  collections of oral literature of  cultural traditions epic poetry songs  myths whether they be on hard disk on  tape recorder and thinking about how  those collections might best be  preserved protected so that they will  also be able to transmit to the next  generation and then also to disseminate  those when appropriate online in print  on air so we're building collections on  an offline of these incredible oral  traditions we're also running training  workshops for community members and  scholars to come together to come up  with the best practices about how to  document carefully we run a publications  program occasional papers that we print  as well as a partnership now with a  cambridge-based publisher the open book  publishers to try and disseminate  materials like this online and also we  give out grants have the most important  thing we do is get out small grants  stimulus grants seed corn money to  particularly community members involved  in documentation projects we've given  out 18 grants now and we have  collections from 30 different countries  on our website let's talk a little bit  about the work you are doing to capture  these oral cultures and then I'd also  like to talk a little bit about some  cultures that perhaps have been lost  forever so do you actually send people  out into the world to try and collect  this information partly and then also I  guess it's communities coming to you  looking for money to help documents that  the oral cultures as well that's quite  right I don't believe that it's a good  model to send people out from the West  you need to go and collect documents i  think the partnerships what we're trying  to support there are lots of grants out  there for university-based scholars in  the US the UK and europe to apply for  money for fieldwork we're interested in  the people who fall through those gaps  they can't apply that state of the  national science foundation or the  British Academy because they don't  adhere or they're not part of any  National University in their own home  country we believe that is the elegant  partnerships that we can support by  which may be foreign scholars are  working deeply in association with the  community the community want to do  something they have a cultural center  there is a local oral knowledge holder  who may be eager to share their  knowledge and have it documented and  that triangulation of research between  maybe a foreign scholar who provides  support training resources a local  community who are actively engaged and  also people in the field who have the  knowledge they want to share that's not  what time is all right excellent that's  can you give us a specific example of  something recently that you've worked on  we've worked a lot in Asia in India and  Nepal and also cultural Tibetan parts of  China partly because many of the board  members of the project have research  areas and interests there so we've  supported a project in central India  whereby a community member wanted to do  work didn't have the technology  themselves to to actually do careful  documentation got some training through  our project got a small grant and is  working with a local anthropologist and  they are collecting amazing stories  about a performance of sort of snake  worship that lasts all night long and  they have been committing that material  to our website also publishing through  us so it's a kind of stimulus grant to  support people who are doing great work  who otherwise might not get the access  okay and let's be clear by oral culture  what do you mean by that okay well  that's a very nice question in a way and  let's make it more complex by saying  oral literature it's a difficult term it  doesn't trip off the tongue but also  it's a term that setting do for some  rehabilitation we have to realize that  most of the work  world's cultures how are transmitted  orally and languages are not just about  words and grammar but they're about the  vehicles for the transmission of  cultural knowledge oral literature and  oral culture encompass all those forms  of verbal art that if you don't have a  writing system if you don't have a  traditionally developed writing system  perhaps where you would commit your  knowledge and your history to paper then  it's all encoded orally so songs poetry  proverbs trickster rhymes epic poetry  and all kinds of genres of oral  performance and those things can relate  to the environment to agriculture to  history and knowledge I've worked very  closely with shaman's in the Himalayas  in Nepal in particular who really are  the repositories of all knowledge about  the community but also about the past so  how do you capture that information do  you videotape it you record it all of  the above one of the most exciting  things that's happening i think is the  revolution in access to Internet  technology and generally digital  technology on the one hand of course  globalization and whatever that may be  maybe one of the processes that is  eroding the diversity of human cultural  expressions around the world we think  all the time about people on the fringes  on the margins being sort of pushed to  the sides further at the same time  fairly cheap air travel a better  communications through Skype and email  and Internet mean that people have  access for the first time to quite  affordable digital technology one of the  things that we've seen communities  interested in is not having to commit  their oral knowledge to print which can  somehow lock it down into one genre and  perhaps even ossify it and make it  difficult to transmit again so digital  technology videos you say an audio  recording is a very important part of  what we're doing it allows three or four  different performances to live in  parallel and not one of them to be  committed as standard okay are there  oral cultures today that are endangered  of of going away forever and once they  do go away is it possible to bring them  back somehow many of the world's oral  cultures and the languages that convey  them are endangered and at risk  whether it's possible to bring them back  is a much more complex question I'm  sorry let me interrupt and they're  endangered because the people are dying  off and and why are they indeed there  are magnets for languages to be  endangered and then the cultures that  are associated with them most languages  become endangered because the speaker's  themselves transition to speaking  another language they move out of their  tribal or regional or ethnic language  into the maybe more a national or  international language another thing  that does happen is the communities  themselves can be completely wiped out  whether it be through war famine  migration or natural disasters and we  saw that during the tsunami when small  communities were completely destroyed at  the same time sometimes languages can  bounce back there's a wonderful story  actually of a project in the u.s.  working in Oklahoma with the Miami tribe  who have kind of reclaimed and  revitalized language that probably was  on the cusp of disappearance I don't  believe that it's for scholars to revive  languages or save them that's not really  a term that we should use it is however  communities who save languages and  support their own transmission and  scholars can partner with them and work  with them to make sure those stories  succeed there are all the time stories  of languages and cultures disappearing  without record vanishing voices last  year their language ball which is spoken  on the Andaman Islands disappeared when  the last speaker died there's a language  in nepal by the name of couse under  which may have a couple of speakers left  but essentially transmission where you  still have children who speak it has  completely ceased so those languages  really are on their last legs and have  has the project done any work with those  two examples in fact not those two  languages but we are supporting a  scholar senior Indian academic who's  coming to give a lecture about one of  those languages she's worked for the  last 20 years in that community what are  some of the greatest challenges that the  project phases there are many challenges  there are logistical challenges in terms  of deploying our resources and our  knowledge appropriately in the field  many of the places that we work our  places of political turmoil and strife  they can be very remote two or three  days walk from a road so communications  can be difficult I think and other  challenges  really telling the story to an audience  of people who are interested I'm always  surprised by how many people react to  the story of endangered languages a kind  of neo-darwinian logic oh well if people  aren't using them just let them die is  often invoked but those very same people  who are against the documentation of  let's say endangered culture endangered  language are interested in their local  church an old building they want to  restore that they may be very concerned  by global biodiversity and some of the  endangered species that we know about on  the planet so I think we have a mission  really to connect with the wider  audience of practice often outside of  the universities as much as inside  people who are committed to this kind of  work need the support and have unique  collections they want to share why is it  important that we not let these  languages die off why should we care I  mean to your point I grew up  multilingual my mother is Dutch and my  father was Italian so I grew up already  speaking a number of languages I think  multilingualism has been the norm for  most of the world over most of history  and monolingualism namely speaking one  language and increasingly that's  something like English let's say is a  historical aberration it hasn't been the  case for most people over most of  history that they speak one soul  language I believe that making sense of  understanding and appreciating our  cultural and linguistic diversity is  part of what makes us human in fact some  people would argue that language the  potential for language and our ability  to speak at children learn it without  even trying is one of the defining  features of our humanity one of my  colleagues said that it would be an  absolute disaster if linguistics went  down as one of the only disciplines in  science to preside over the demise of  its own subject matter and do nothing we  have to act very good thank you so much  for being here with us today and sharing  some of your work it's a real pleasure  thank you for more information about  professor turin and his research please  visit our website at yale edu backslash  macmillan report be sure to join us  again for another episode of the  macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  you

